{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>A good name.</b> This verse too, \"a good name,\" is joined to the preceding verses, for the poor man who knows his deeds are good — a name is more honorable to him than good oil and more pleasurable. And \"the day of death\" is good for the one who has a good name, more than the day of his birth, for on the day of his birth no person knows what he will be, whether good or bad, but at his death his name is proclaimed and it becomes known that he was a good man. It is also possible that \"and the day of death\" is good for the possessor of the good name because he will find rest and good reward and will not see toil — unlike the day of his birth, for man is born to toil. Some interpret \"and the day of death\" to mean a person is obligated to bring the day of death to mind more than the day of his birth, but this matter itself is good, so it is not the meaning of the verse."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "The Hebrew וא' (before רע) is taken as the conjunction 'or'; it could alternatively be an abbreviation for אם ('whether'), which would yield 'whether good or bad' without the disjunctive 'or.'"}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:28:01.644439606Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}